# M6 — posture rule 7 (NO TRUNCATE) made unreachable.
#
# V15: TRUNCATE bypasses row-level BEFORE UPDATE/DELETE triggers, so decision_revision's
# append-only guarantee rests on BEFORE TRUNCATE statement triggers AND on the absence of
# the TRUNCATE grant. Rule 7 is the check on the second half. Like rule 3, it had no
# control when the adversarial pass ran and `if false then` left the suite green at 44/44.
# WHY `authenticator` AND NOT `__never_a_role__`, measured rather than guessed:
# `has_table_privilege` on a role that does not exist RAISES
# `role "__never_a_role__" does not exist`. That makes verify_security_posture()
# itself throw, so the migration's closing posture call fails, the template is never
# built, and the suite runs ZERO tests — the gate reported MISATTRIBUTED because the
# named control could not run, which was accurate. M5 gets away with the same
# placeholder because its predicate is a STRING COMPARISON
# (`pg_get_userbyid(...) in (...)`), not a catalog lookup.
# `authenticator` exists on both Supabase and the emulation and holds no table
# privileges, so the clause is unmatchable while the function still runs.
id: M6
title: posture rule 7 NO TRUNCATE replaced by `if false then`
expect: caught
proves: posture rule 7 (canon 4.8 rule 7, V15) has a control
expect-test: [M6] raises ST099 for TRUNCATE granted to authenticated on decision_revision

file: supabase/migrations/20261021140500_verify_security_posture.sql
count: 1
@@FIND
      and has_table_privilege('authenticated', c.oid, 'TRUNCATE')
@@REPLACE
      and has_table_privilege('authenticator', c.oid, 'TRUNCATE')
@@END
